                                estimated_seconds, origin, status, parent_question_id, variation_type, ai_review, passage_group, passage_position, verified_by, subject_group_id, specialisation_id, image_url)
  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'How do vaccines protect the body against disease? Differentiate active and passive immunity with one example of each. (3 marks)', 'A vaccine contains antigens - killed or weakened (attenuated) microorganisms, inactivated toxins (toxoids) or parts of the organism. When given, it stimulates the immune system to produce antibodies and memory B and T cells without causing the disease. If the person is later exposed to the real organism, the memory cells recognise it and produce a rapid, strong response that prevents illness. When most people are vaccinated, spread is interrupted (herd immunity).
Active immunity: the person''s own immune system makes antibodies after infection or vaccination. It takes time to develop but lasts long, often for life. Examples: immunity after measles infection; vaccination with BCG, measles-rubella or hepatitis B vaccine.
Passive immunity: ready-made antibodies are given to the person. It acts immediately but lasts only weeks or months. Examples: maternal antibodies passing to the baby through the placenta and breast milk; injection of rabies immunoglobulin or anti-tetanus serum after exposure.', null, '[{"point": "A vaccine contains killed/weakened microbes, toxoids or their parts (antigens); it stimulates the immune system to make antibodies and memory cells without causing the disease", "marks": 1}, {"point": "On later exposure memory cells respond quickly and strongly, preventing illness; high coverage also gives herd immunity", "marks": 0.5}, {"point": "Active immunity: the body makes its own antibodies - long-lasting, slow to develop (natural infection, vaccination e.g. BCG, measles)", "marks": 0.75}, {"point": "Passive immunity: ready-made antibodies are received - immediate but short-lived (maternal antibodies via placenta/breast milk, antitetanus or anti-rabies serum/immunoglobulin)", "marks": 0.75}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
