  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Describe the structure of human skin and state its main functions. (3 marks)', 'Structure: the skin is the largest organ of the body and has two main layers. The outer epidermis is stratified squamous epithelium; its surface layer (stratum corneum) is made of dead keratinised cells, and its deepest layer contains dividing cells and melanocytes that make melanin. Below it, the dermis is dense connective tissue containing blood vessels, nerve endings and receptors, hair follicles, sweat glands and sebaceous (oil) glands. Beneath the skin lies the subcutaneous layer (hypodermis) of fat.
Functions: protects the body against microorganisms, chemicals, injury and ultraviolet light; prevents water loss; regulates body temperature through sweating and changes in skin blood flow; contains receptors for touch, pain, pressure and temperature; excretes small amounts of salts and urea in sweat; makes vitamin D in sunlight; stores fat as an energy reserve and insulator.', null, '[{"point": "Structure: epidermis (outer stratified squamous, keratinised; stratum corneum and basal layer with melanocytes) and dermis (connective tissue with blood vessels, nerve endings, hair follicles, sweat and sebaceous glands); hypodermis with fat below", "marks": 1.5}, {"point": "Functions (any five): protection against microbes, injury and UV; temperature regulation (sweat, blood flow); sensation (touch, pain, heat); excretion of salts/urea in sweat; synthesis of vitamin D; storage of fat; prevents water loss", "marks": 1.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
